# [[.Name]]

A starter Goa / Goa-AI service created by `surla init` or the `init_project`
MCP tool, with a starter evaluator suite that `surla verify` and `verify_change`
can run against it.

## What is here

- `design/` declares the `[[.Name]]` service. `ask` (POST /ask) runs the
  `helper` agent on a question. `price` (GET /prices/{item}) returns an item's
  price in cents. The agent can call `price` through its `lookup_price` tool.
  `override_prompt` (PUT /prompts/{prompt_id}) replaces the helper's system
  prompt text until the process exits; it requires the key in the
  `SURLA_LIVE_TARGET_KEY` environment variable, which Surla sets for a live
  target, and rejects every call without one. `key` is left optional in
  `design/design.go` so a missing `X-Live-Target-Key` header reaches
  `Service.APIKeyAuth` and returns `401` (`unauthorized`) rather than `400`
  (`missing_field`) from the HTTP decoder; if you pass a security credential
  field to tools with `Inject(...)` (which requires the payload field to be in
  `Required(...)`), check for a missing header in HTTP middleware before the
  generated decoder runs when you want missing headers to answer `401`.
  `design/model.go` declares the C4 architecture model. `design/story.go`
  declares the Story: who uses the service and the flows their requests take
  (see "Change checkpoints" below).
- `gen/` is generated from `design/`. After changing `design/`, call the
  `generate_code` MCP tool when connected to the Surla project server (or run `surla gen`
  in this directory): it runs Goa's generator for this module inside Surla's
  sandbox. Never edit `gen/`. Generation compiles the design only, so update the
  hand-written code in `internal/` and `cmd/` to match it, then build and test
  your uncommitted files with `check_project` as described in "Next steps".
  `AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md` is generated with it and maps the agent, its tools
  and the generated packages for coding agents; it names the same Surla tools
  as "Next steps" for generating, checking and verifying this project.
- `internal/helper` is the agent's planner and `internal/service` implements the
  service. `Service.Ask` builds an in-memory runtime and store per question
  because this starter has no multi-turn sessions or `Confirmation` tools, and
  `Service.Price` returns expected outcomes as a result value (`known: false`)
  because errors returned by bound service methods that do not implement
  `planner.ToolFailureProvider` end the agent run. If you add a tool with
  `Confirmation` (which pauses the run before the bound method's own
  authorization runs; the host can also deny unauthorized calls immediately
  when handling the suspension), build one runtime and store in `main` so
  paused runs can be resumed, as Surla's `examples/orders` reference service
  does.
- `cmd/[[.Name]]` serves the API. The agent runs in memory and sends every
  model call to a goa-ai model gateway given with `-model-gateway host:port`;
  the gateway holds the model provider and its API keys.
- `eval/` is a separate Go module: the starter evaluator suite. It runs the
  `[[.Name]]` binary in Surla's sandbox and serves a scripted model (not an
  LLM) through its own model gateway. Its three scenarios are:
  - `prices_tea`: asks "How much is tea?" and checks that the agent called
    `lookup_price` and answered from that call's result;
  - `unknown_item`: asks "How much is coffee?", which the
    catalog does not price, and checks that the agent calls `lookup_price`
    and reports that it found no price;
  - `prompt_override_reaches_model`: checks that `override_prompt` rejects a
    missing or wrong key and that the next question's model request carries the
    new prompt text. Its `--live` mode starts the
  `[[.Name]]` for the Surla project server's `start_live_target`, planning with the model
  the Surla project server was started with. Its program,
  `eval/gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals`, is generated with the rest of `eval/gen/`
  from `eval/design/` and calls `eval/acceptance`, the package you write: its
  `Name`, `Hooks`, `New`, `Live` and `LiveThroughGateway`. After changing
  `eval/design/`, regenerate its code with
  `surla gen -dir eval` (or call `generate_code` with `target: "eval"`).

The evaluator suite lives in this repository, so anyone who can read the code
can read its scenarios; none of them is hidden. Keep scenarios that must stay
hidden from builders in an evaluator repository they cannot read.

## Next steps

If you created this project with `surla init`, make the first commit and start
Surla in this directory:

```sh
git init
git add .gitignore README.md AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md go.mod go.sum design gen internal cmd eval
git commit -m "Start [[.Name]] from surla init"
surla
```

`surla` starts this project's server, or attaches to it when it is already
running, and opens the project's views page in your browser. If the guide
created this project with `init_project`, the Surla project server has already
initialized the Git repository, created the initial commit (`commit` in
`init_project`'s result), bound itself to this directory (`dir`), and pinned
the evaluator suite to that initial commit.

For each change:

1. The guide describes the change with `propose_change`, and with
   `revise_change` when it changes. You confirm it on the views page, which
   also shows its architecture preview (`preview_architecture`).
2. The builder edits the code. When it edits `design/`, it first calls
   `generate_code` (with `target: "eval"` when it edits `eval/design/`), then
   updates the hand-written code to match.
3. The builder calls `check_project` to build and test its uncommitted files,
   this module and `eval/`, in Linux containers (Docker required). When it
   cannot download dependencies and its output shows a missing `go.sum` or
   `go.work.sum` entry or a `go.mod` update, it calls `update_dependencies` and
   checks again. `read_check_output` returns the captured output, page by
   page, for the `op_id` in either result.
4. The builder commits, then passes the new commit ID as `candidate_commit` to
   `start_live_target` and `verify_change`.

See the
[MCP tools reference](https://github.com/goadesign/surla/blob/main/docs/REFERENCE.md#mcp-tools)
for each tool's inputs and results.

`surla verify` and `surla approve` are operator tools that check a commit
outside the server. To verify the current commit against the starter
evaluator suite yourself:

```sh
VERIFY_DIR="$(mktemp -d)"
WORK_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/work"
REPORT_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/reports"
surla verify \
  -candidate-repo . -candidate-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -binary [[.Name]]=./cmd/[[.Name]] \
  -suite-repo . -suite-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -suite-module eval -suite-command ./gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals \
  -request-revision starter -work-dir "$WORK_DIR" -report-dir "$REPORT_DIR" -builder-view
```

`surla verify` keeps `$WORK_DIR` after the run: it holds the clones, the
built binaries, and the evaluator's private evidence, including each build's
log and each step's output in `$WORK_DIR/evidence/`. `-report-dir "$REPORT_DIR"`
always writes the full verification report outside the repository as
`$REPORT_DIR/<digest>.json` (used by `surla approve`), while `-builder-view` hides holdout scenario names and checks
in the completed report JSON and summary line (if a run fails before writing a
report, `stderr` prints operator setup/execution failure diagnostics). Do not
share `$WORK_DIR`, `$REPORT_DIR`, or setup/execution failure output with coding
agents that build the service.

`surla verify` checks a commit. The builder runs the `check_project` loop in
"Next steps" above before it commits.

## Change checkpoints

Surla reads this workspace's `design/*.go` privately when `propose_change` saves
a Change Card. The saved source contributes to `request_revision`; callers do
not upload it. The guide calls `refresh_story` to evaluate the workspace's
domain Story and passes its succeeded `op_id` as `story_refresh_op_id` to adopt
the Story and matching source.
An adopted Story remains paired with its source when a later revision omits
`story_refresh_op_id`.

The Story is `design/story.go`. It ships with one finished flow,
`prices-cookie`, and with the test that flow requires: the method
`PricesCookie` of package `eval/acceptance`. To describe new behavior, the
guide adds a flow to that file and calls `refresh_story`. When you start a
build of the change, each finished flow of its Story is a test the build
requires, and the test writer adds the missing methods beside `PricesCookie`.

To try prompt wording on a running `[[.Name]]` before it is verified, the
builder calls `start_live_target` with the full candidate commit ID, then
`draft_override` with `prompt_id` `[[.Name]].helper.system`. To keep a draft,
the builder writes the text into `internal/helper/planner.go`, runs
`check_project`, commits, and calls `verify_change` with the new commit ID and
the confirmed Change Card's `request_revision`.
